    "result_text": "The build passes with the test green. Let me summarize the change.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe `householdId` is now deterministic and derived from `(lastName, postcode)`, and `sharesHousehold` only bypasses the duplicate block. Four files touched, each edited as a small, self-contained unit:\n\n1. **`AssignHousehold.java`** \u2014 Now assigns *every* owner a `householdId` = first 12 hex chars of SHA-256 over `normalize(lastName) + '|' + postcode`. Dropped the `sharesHousehold` gate, the repository dependency, and the back-fill loop, so the class shrank to one simple hashing step.\n\n2. **`EnsureUniqueHousehold.java`** \u2014 Rewired to key off the computed `householdId` (comparing `owner.getHouseholdId()` against existing owners' ids) instead of re-deriving from last name + address. Still bypassed when `sharesHousehold: true`, throwing the existing `DuplicateHouseholdException` (\u2192 409) otherwise.\n\n3. **`FlagPossibleDuplicate.java`** \u2014 Soft-matches on shared `householdId` (+ different telephone), and skips flagging entirely when `sharesHousehold: true`, so a declared household member is never marked a suspected duplicate.\n\n4. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 Inserted the `ensureUniqueHousehold` step *after* `assignHousehold` (so the id is populated before the block compares) and before `ensureUniqueIdentity`.\n\nBehavior now: two owners with the same last name and postcode share a `householdId` automatically; a second such owner is a 409 household duplicate unless it sets `sharesHousehold`, in which case it is created as a declared member and not flagged.\n\nOn the membership-level clause: the only `membershipLevel` input is `namesakeCount` (documented and computed by first+last name in `CountNamesakes`/`OwnerMapper`), which does not key off the household \u2014 there is no household-size term feeding membership in this codebase, so nothing needed redirecting there. Duplicate detection is the only thing that keyed off the household, and it now uses the computed `householdId`.\n\nStructural impact was kept low by having the duplicate-detection functions compare the already-computed `householdId` string rather than introducing a shared hashing helper \u2014 no new files, and each changed method stays trivial (low CC, small surrounding class complexity).",
